"It's not difficult to predict, especially about the future" was said by a 
Danish guy some 70 years ago.

Today I read a "summary" in the Danish version of Computerworld that ".. Compaq 
has Tru64 Unix on Alpha, NonStop Kernel on MIPS and OpenVMS on VAX..."

That's what the IT-press knows about the glorius purchases of Tandem and 
Digital some years ago.

And that tells what Compaq has done to it: Forgot it in their struggle for 
retaining their no. one PC position which they lost anyway.

I thought, that the Compaq marketing combined with the strong innovation and 
engineering of DEC would become a smahing hit. There you see how wring one can 
be.

Fortunately, HP knows much more about former DEC than Compaq did and does. I'm 
pretty sure, they (HP) know exactly, what they have purchased. The DEC part of 
Compaq and HP are companies very much alike.

So the only thing that puzzles me, is why Compaq got rid of Alpha in june. Was 
this part of the plan together with HP?

And if yes, why?

Rgds
