Here is an interesting article that details the foothold that Linux is
gaining in movie industry.  This is especially true with professional
animators.

http://cgw.pennnet.com/Articles/Article_Display.cfm?Section=Articles&Subsect
ion=Display&ARTICLE_ID=118664


I first got into the Alpha market with Linux many years ago because I needed
a cheap reliable web server.  I bought a used multia and never looked back.
6 Alpha machine later I'm still having fun with old equipment!  A little
later a lot of animator friends of mine were asking me about AlphaNT because
of the excellent software that they had available to them.  I even installed
NT on a Alpha Linux box to see how it would go (it went well).  Then in
typical fashion someone *ahem* dropped the ball when it seemed that the
alpha boxes were starting to make some inroads.

Now using my circular rampage argument method I'll try to get back to the
point; it would be nice to see how a modern Alpha machine would perform when
coupled with some of the software packages mentioned in the article.  I
think it would make a splendid combination, if only the platform would stick
around for a little while it might actually *gasp* become popular!


Here's to pipe dreams and processor schemes

Aaron
http://www.italian-cars.com




