Previously we had experienced poor ide hard drive performance with the
UP2000+ due to the Cypress Southbridge.  Recently while testing the API
CS20 with Redhat 7.1 we discovered similarly poor hdparm performance.  I
later traced this problem to the configureation of the default kernel
shipped with RH 7.1 Alpha (2.4.3).  The kernel needs to be recompiled
with support for the ALi15x3 chipset so that DMA support can be
enabled.  Doing so broke the network drivers (eepro100).  To fix that I
needed to force the eepro100 driver to be compiled into the kernel
rather than loaded as a module.

Before the recompile:
hdparm -c3 -m16 -u1 /dev/hda only yielded 2.07 MB/s (with -t) because
-d1 couldn't be used.

After the compile I didn't even need to use hdparm to get 7.78 MB/s
(with -t).  This compile allows the use of -d1 on the drive.
Performance may still seem slow but that's due to the drive's
limitations (3600rpm IBM-DCAA-34330 theoretical peak of 16MB/s) not the
chipset's.

This works for both 2.4.3 supplied by RH and 2.4.9 (from kernel.org).
See axp-kernel-list for my post yesterday on problems with pc_keyb.c
being broken for alpha in 2.4.9 and the work around.

-Keith

