Just in case you didn't think of it already: If you have a boot partition 1,
where the kernel resides, and which is to be mounted as /boot (that's what
I'd recommend anyway), you should use
0:1/vmlinux-2.2.17-4 root=/dev/hda3
1:1/vmlinux-2.2.17-4 root=/dev/hda3 single
instead of
> > 0:1/boot/vmlinux-2.2.17-4 root=/dev/hda3
> > 1:1/boot/vmlinux-2.2.17-4 root=/dev/hda3 single
because in that case /boot is just the name of the mount point, and the
kernel is at the top level of disk partition 1.

regards
	Oliver




