Dear Alpha-Linux users !

This is a "success" message, intended for all who may need it 
and for the archives. 
I would like to use this occasion to thank all those who sent hints, 
especially Jay Estabrook's answer gave me much insight.


I was having problems with my AlphaStation 200 4/166 ("Avanti"): 
After replacing my old Digital TGA 8-plane graphics card "ZLXp-E1" 
with a Matrox Millennium G200 SD ("G2+/MSDP/8I" - the PCI version), 
the machine would not boot any more - the screen was totally black, 
no ARC text showed up, let alone any Milo and/or Linux messages. 
The machine had ARC (3.5-13), LinuxLoader 1.5, and Milo 2.0.35-c5.6.

Jay recommended an ARC firmware update, he commented and explained 
this behaviour as follows:

| Sounds like the BIOS emulator in the ARC console doesn't make it
| through the BIOS code on the new card. This is bad... :-\
| 
| The reason that BIOS emulation became necessary is *because* the
| more recent cards do NOT power up in anything close to standard
| VGA mode. Their BIOS code MUST be run to get them into that state.


After this info, I decided to upgrade the ARC firmware version. 
As we also run Tru64 machines, I do have the "Alpha Firmware Update" 
CDs at hand, and they contain both the SRM and the ARC console. 
(Firmware can also be downloadad from 'ftp.digital.com'.) 
For completeness' sake, I upgraded both (my AS 200 has both SRM 
and ARC, but I use only ARC), ARC is now at version 4.58.

In this version 4.58, ARC can handle the Matrox Millennium G200, 
so now again the machine boots (and works). 
In Linux (kernel 2.2.16, SuSE 7.0), the XF86 "SVGA" server 
("xsvga-3.3.6-85") handles the card - no problems till now.


Additional notes:

Guido Serassio and Jeff Donsbach had commented on the possibility 
to do the firmware upgrade from ARC. However, the manual for my CD 
(firmware 5.4) had a "caution" note to use SRM because some ISA 
devices might need re-setting if the previous firmware was too old; 
as I did not know that CD version, I followed the note and succeeded.

Rick van der Linde proposed a flash of the graphic card's BIOS 
might help - I do not know, and I will not try that because the 
ARC upgrade solved my problem.


Again, thank you for all your help.

Joerg Bruehe

-- 
Joerg Bruehe, SQL Datenbanksysteme GmbH, Berlin, Germany
     (speaking only for himself)
mailto: joerg@sql.de




