On Fri, 28 Sep 2001 02:01, you wrote:
> I seem to be getting a weird problem with eth0. It seems to work OK
> for small amounts of data, but as soon as I try to ftp anything it
> seems to just hang.

Could be the ipv.4 vs ipv.6 thig -try this for an immediate fix:

echo 0 > /proc/sys/net/ipv4/tcp_sack
echo 0 > /proc/sys/net/ipv4/tcp_timestamps
echo 0 > /proc/sys/net/ipv4/tcp_window_scaling

For a a permanent fix, the values need to be set on boot in /etc/sysctl.conf. 
Here's the relevant extract:

----------------begin /et/sysctl.conf---------------

# Disables packet forwarding
net.ipv4.ip_forward = 0
#
# Enables source route verification
net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter = 1
#
# Disables automatic defragmentation (needed for masquerading, LVS)
net.ipv4.ip_always_defrag = 0
#
# Disables the magic-sysrq key
kernel.sysrq = 0
#
# Turn off rfc1323 stuff which stuffs connections under 10Mbs
net.ipv4.tcp_timestamps = 0
net.ipv4.tcp_ window_scaling = 0
net.ipv4.tcp_sack = 0
#
# Change a coupla bits to more respectable values
net.ipv4.tcp_fin_timeout = 2
net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time = 2

----------------end /et/sysctl.conf---------------

And, in the words of the bass fisherman who gave this to me --happy 
networking!
-- 
john in syd




