> On Fri, 28 Sep 2001, Ken Siersma wrote:
>
> > free:
> >              total       used       free     shared    buffers     cached
> > Mem:       2056616    2052304       4312          0       2240     887328
> > -/+ buffers/cache:    1162736     893880
> > Swap:      2047984    1234536     813448
> >
> > granted, I don't typically look to 'free' in these situations.  what do you make of
> > it?
>
> Looks like it's hitting swap pretty heavily (to the tune of 1.2GB).  That
> would DEFINITELY explain a major slowdown since disk access is not nearly
> as fast as physical RAM.  Also, any I/O activity involved in using swap
> will cause delays in any other I/O that other binaries may need to
> perform.  Of course, handling swap is usually a higher priority than
> normal processes, so that would explain some pre-emption.
>
> C

